1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,530
Many of us were told from an early age that, be it at school or in life, "you learn from your mistakes".
我们很多人从小就被告知，无论是在学校还是生活中，“吃一堑，长一智”。

2
00:00:09,530 --> 00:00:11,980
But is this actually true?
但这话真的成立吗？

3
00:00:11,980 --> 00:00:18,420
The short answer is 'yes' – failure can be a teachable moment.
简短的答案是“是的”——失败可以成为一个具有教育意义的时刻。

4
00:00:18,420 --> 00:00:26,400
But learning from our mistakes is, in reality, very hard because we don't like to fail.
但事实上，从错误中学习并非易事，因为我们都不喜欢失败。

5
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:42,640
It doesn't feel good, so we react to failure in impulsive and emotional ways, like giving up on a task prematurely, telling ourselves we don't care whether we succeed, or finding fault with the task itself.
失败的感觉并不好，所以我们会以冲动且情绪化的方式应对它，比如过早放弃一项任务、告诉自己不在乎成败，或是批评任务本身就有问题。

6
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:53,240
This is self-protective, according to Hallgeir Sjåstad, a professor of psychology and leadership at the Norwegian School of Economics.
挪威经济学院心理学与领导力教授哈尔盖尔·肖斯塔德表示，这种反应是一种自我保护。

7
00:00:53,240 --> 00:01:05,290
"Most of us want to think of ourselves as competent and capable", he says, so when we fail "it poses a serious threat to our self-image".
他说：“我们大多数人都希望认为自己是有能力、能胜任的”，因此当我们失败时，“这会对我们的自我形象构成严重威胁”。

8
00:01:05,290 --> 00:01:15,590
Fortunately, there is research to suggest that there are some strategies to help us overcome the emotional barriers around failure.
幸运的是，有研究表明，存在一些策略可以帮助我们克服围绕失败产生的情绪障碍。

9
00:01:15,590 --> 00:01:20,780
One of them is to adopt a third-person perspective.
其中之一是采用第三人称视角。

10
00:01:20,780 --> 00:01:28,520
Instead of asking "Why did I fail?", we could ask "Why did Sam fail?", for example.
比如，不要问“我为什么失败了？”，而是问“山姆为什么失败了？”。

11
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:46,840
Multiple studies by psychologist Ethan Kross at the University of Michigan show that adopting a third-person perspective helps to soften our negative emotional reactions, allowing us to look at failure more objectively.
密歇根大学心理学家伊桑·克罗斯开展的多项研究显示，采用第三人称视角有助于缓和我们的负面情绪反应，让我们能更客观地看待失败。

12
00:01:46,840 --> 00:01:54,850
A second strategy involves offering advice to others who may be in the same position as us.
第二种策略是为那些可能和我们处于相同境遇的人提供建议。

13
00:01:54,850 --> 00:02:09,260
This strategy led to better levels of motivation and academic success in the test groups – involving both adults and children – that were asked to give advice based on their own failures.
在一项涉及成人和儿童的测试中，那些被要求根据自己的失败经历给出建议的组别，展现出了更高的积极性和学业成就。

14
00:02:09,260 --> 00:02:22,770
Professors Eskreis-Winkler, Fishbach and Duckworth found that the satisfaction of helping others "forces people to engage with their experience and what they have learned".
埃斯克雷斯-温克勒、菲什巴赫和达克沃斯三位教授发现，帮助他人带来的满足感“会促使人们反思自己的经历以及从中收获的教训”。

15
00:02:22,770 --> 00:02:28,750
The writer Samuel Beckett once said: "fail again, fail better".
作家塞缪尔·贝克特曾说过：“再试一次，败得更漂亮些。”

16
00:02:28,750 --> 00:02:34,540
But it now seems that we should be saying: "fail again, fail smarter".
但现在我们似乎应该说：“再试一次，败得更明智些。”

17
00:02:34,540 --> 00:02:47,240
Failure is an inevitable part of life, but by learning to overcome the emotional barriers around it, we may find the road to success is a little easier to navigate.
失败是人生中不可避免的一部分，但通过学会克服其带来的情绪障碍，我们或许能让通往成功的道路变得更容易一些。
